@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Credential boundary — inject, strip, sentinel (every tier but open+forward)

participant "cmd/proveo run" as RUN <<app>>
participant "internal/provider\nDetect · Resolve" as PROV <<app>>
database "<stateDir>/inject/broker.env\n0600, outside every agent mount" as SECRET <<error>>
participant "proveo-egress\nMITM + internal/broker" as MITM <<app>>
participant "agent process" as AGENT <<app>>
participant "pinned provider host" as VENDOR <<cloud>>
participant "any other host" as OTHER <<cloud>>

RUN ARROW_MAIN PROV : detect provider from keys present
PROV ARROW_MAIN RUN : exactly one provider → broker on
RUN ARROW_ERROR SECRET : write real key (host-side)
RUN ARROW_MAIN AGENT : " ""-e ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=proveo-brokered"" \n""-e PROVEO_CREDENTIAL_BROKER_KEYS=…"" "
note over AGENT
  The agent holds a **sentinel**, not a secret.
  ""apply_broker_sentinel"" rewrites any residual value a
  mounted file re-loaded, so reading its own environment
  yields nothing usable.
end note

SECRET ARROW_MAIN MITM : read once at startup
AGENT ARROW_MAIN MITM : request (TLS decrypted here —\nthe only point where it can be)

alt destination == pinned provider
  MITM ARROW_CLOUD VENDOR : successLabel(inject real auth header)
else any other destination
  MITM ARROW_ERROR OTHER : errorLabel(strip authorization · x-api-key ·\nx-goog-api-key · api-key · proxy-authorization)
end

note over MITM
  **"Inject keys, never expose"** — the principle is borrowed from
  omnigent's credential_proxy, adapted to a fact that changes its
  shape here: the **vendor CLI**, not this harness, makes the model
  call. So the secret is confined to this hop, injected on the
  provider's own hosts and stripped everywhere else.

  **Header-strip is the common path, not a hard guarantee.** A key the
  agent read from a mounted "".env"" cannot leave //through those
  headers//, but a secret re-encoded into a request body evades a
  header strip entirely. The DLP scan
  (""_spec/internal/egresspolicy/egress-policy-layers\.puml"") is the
  complementary layer, and neither is absolute on its own — which is
  why the mounted-"".env"" warning below exists at all rather than being
  made redundant by this hop.
end note

note over RUN
  **Gate:** the broker arms only when exactly **one**
  provider is detected (or a manifest pins one). A
  multi-key environment leaves the agent with a sentinel
  and nothing behind it — indistinguishable from a bad key,
  so ""proveo run"" **warns** in that case, naming the
  detected providers. ""provider.DetectVars()"" is the
  superset to clear when arranging a single-provider run:
  it includes names that are detected but not brokerable
  (bedrock ""AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID"", vertex
  ""GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS""), so an unrelated cloud
  credential silently suppresses brokering.
end note

note over AGENT, MITM
  **Mode-bound.** ""broker""/""proxy"" cannot decrypt TLS, so
  they keep key-in-env behavior with honest warnings; a
  key in a *mounted* "".env"" is still readable as a file
  there. Full isolation = firewall + host-env provisioning.
end note

note over MITM
  **A request matches at most one route, and only that route's secret can
  be injected.** Routes are matched by destination host, so a provider's
  key is never attached to a request bound for a different provider —
  the property that makes holding several keys at once safe. Host
  matching is dot-anchored: "".foo\.com"" and ""foo\.com"" both match
  ""api\.foo\.com"", ""evil-foo\.com"" matches neither. Policy classifies
  provider hosts the same way, deliberately.

  First match wins, and that is a choice rather than an accident:
  registry hosts are disjoint today, and if an entry ever overlapped, the
  earlier — more specific, registry-ordered — route must win instead of
  the outcome depending on map order.

  **On-route it injects, or passes the agent's own credential through. It
  never strips on-route**, because the provider must keep its auth.
  Off-route it strips whenever //any// route is configured, even when it
  holds no value to inject.

  That last clause is a fix, not a detail: gating the strip on the broker
  having a usable secret left a key the agent had read from somewhere
  else free to leave via an unbrokered host. Degrading to
  strip-off-route plus pass-through is the safe failure, so a missing or
  empty value file is not an error. An empty ""Config"" yields an inert
  broker whose ""Apply"" is a no-op, which is what makes loading the
  broker unconditionally safe.
end note

note over MITM
  **The on-provider exemption travels on its own axis, and it has to.** The
  route table above is what //injects//; the DLP's list of provider hosts is
  what keeps a credential legal on the way to the vendor. Reading the second
  off the first works only while the two coincide — and ""--credentials
  forward"" is precisely where they do not: the broker is inert, so there are
  no routes, while the agent is carrying the real key by design.

  Empty there, the scan saw ""x-api-key: sk-…"" bound for the provider's own
  API, matched the generic credential shape, and returned ""403 egress
  policy: blocked (secret)"" — the one destination the key exists for was the
  only one denied, and the harness surfaced it as an auth failure. So
  ""proveo run"" states the set outright (""PROVEO_EGRESS_PROVIDER_HOSTS""):
  the detected providers' endpoints **union** the ones the manifest declares.

  The union is not redundancy. A ""subscription: true"" harness logs in
  //inside// the sandbox, so nothing is detectable host-side — the declared
  ""providers:"" list is the only statement of where the token it mints there
  is allowed to go, which makes it load-bearing for exactly the harness that
  has no detected key at all.
end note

note over AGENT, VENDOR
  **""forward"" only carries what the manifest DECLARES, and the gap is not
  symmetric with brokering.** The sentinel loop over ""provider\.KeyVars()""
  runs `if !forwards()`, so under ""--credentials forward"" an //undeclared//
  provider key is neither forwarded by name nor sentinel-injected: it does
  not reach the container at all. ""claudecode"" declares only
  ""CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN"", so ""ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"" + ""forward"" leaves the
  agent with an empty variable, while the same key under ""broker"" works —
  the proxy injects it on-route for every provider it resolved.

  Confirmed from both ends: the in-container probe answers **401** with an
  empty header under ""forward"" and **200** under ""broker"", and
  ""tests/e2e/claudecode_auth_test\.go"" reads the declared set out of the def
  rather than restating it, so the case is skipped for the right reason
  instead of asserting an absence the design never promised.
end note

note over MITM
  **The boundary is testable without buying a completion.** ""GET
  /v1/models"" is authenticated, returns the account's model list, and
  consumes no tokens — so the crossing can be asserted on any //valid//
  credential rather than only a funded one. Driving a real completion made
  an empty credit balance indistinguishable from a broken broker: both
  surfaced as "no file on the host" after the full timeout, and the pane
  blamed auth for a billing problem.

  The same request is issued twice, host-side as a precondition and again
  from inside the container. That pairing is the whole diagnostic: a host
  200 with a container non-200 leaves the egress layer as the only thing
  that changed the answer.
end note

note over SECRET
  Read once at construction; the value may carry its own ""Bearer ""
  prefix. The broker never returns a secret inside an error — an error
  path that echoes what it was handling is how secrets reach logs.
end note

note over RUN
  **Update — the "exactly one provider" gate above is history.**
  ""brokerProviders"" now returns the //whole// detected set and
  ""broker\.Config"" carries one ""Route"" per broker-injectable provider,
  so a multi-provider host is the supported shape rather than the
  warned-about one. The gate that remains is: not ""--credentials
  forward"", the broker not disabled through
  ""PROVEO_CREDENTIAL_BROKER"", and at least one provider resolved.
  The route table shipped: ""internal/broker"" holds N routes with
  ""TestMultiProviderRoutes"" as its regression, and no provider is pinned.
end note

note over RUN
  **A vendor-locked harness out-ranks the detected-key rule.** When the
  manifest pins ""provider:"", that vendor is the only one brokered —
  the other keys on the host are not inference providers //for it// —
  and the pin holds when its key is found anywhere the lookup reaches,
  including the host env rather than a project "".env"". Without that
  exception a developer whose "".env"" holds several vendors could not
  run cursor at all: ""CURSOR_API_KEY"" is typically exported, and
  nothing else about such a host is single-provider.

  Secrets are written up front — before the topology is staged — so the
  broker file exists by the time the MITM starts reading it.
end note

note over RUN
  **Declared-but-missing env is decided before detection, and the order
  is the point.** A key typed at the prompt has to be in hand before
  providers are detected, or it reaches neither the broker nor the
  forwarding decision.

  | harness kind | missing declared env |
  | ""subscription: true"" | warn and run anyway — the agent performs its own login, so an ahead-of-time key prompt asks for a credential the harness never uses |
  | anything else, on a TTY | prompt (the DinD-prompt-shaped wizard), then re-check what is still missing |
  | anything else, no TTY | warn only, naming the variable and its description |

  **The subscription harness's login hint is printed before launch, not
  after.** The variables that were missing at that point are kept aside and
  the hints emitted just before the agent starts — a hint about how to
  authenticate is useless once the operator is already inside a session
  running on the other credential.
end note

note over RUN
  **A keyless role is named before launch, not after the call fails.**
  ""roles\.MissingKeys"" warns per role with both halves of the
  diagnosis — ""ARCHITECT_MODEL=<model> needs <VAR> (<provider>), which
  is not set"". Pointing a role at a provider with no key used to
  surface as the harness's own //"invalid API key"//, which says
  nothing about which of three models was at fault.
end note

note over PROV
  **Where a key is looked up, and why the order is fixed.** The lookup
  prefers the process environment, then one host-side ""KEY=VALUE""
  file: an explicit ""PROVEO_EGRESS_ENV_FILE"" wins, else the project
  "".env"" resolved from the invocation dir, the input dir, then the
  repo root. That file is **host-side only** — it is never mounted into
  the agent on proxy/firewall — and it is resolved //before// the
  missing-env prompts, so a key that exists only in a project "".env""
  is already visible instead of being prompted for twice.

  **Docker's bare ""-e NAME"" forwards only the client process
  environment**, so a secret that lives in that file but was never
  exported is copied into ""proveo""'s own env first
  (""hydrateProcessEnv""); broker mode depends on that hop.
end note

note over PROV
  **Detection is filtered by what the harness declares it can use.**
  ""claudecode"" declares ""providers:[anthropic]"", so listing
  ""OPENAI_API_KEY"" / ""XAI_API_KEY"" / ""GEMINI_API_KEY"" beside it
  implied it could reach them. The auth row already filtered on
  capabilities; the loaded-secrets header line did not, and that is the
  line the operator reads at the confirmation prompt.
end note

note over PROV
  **A provider that accepts more than one credential must not have the
  choice made by declaration order.** ""anthropic"" takes an API key
  //or// a subscription token; whichever the manifest happened to list
  first would otherwise silently win. The ""auth"" row is offered only
  when the operator actually holds more than one of them — a choice is
  shown where one exists, never as decoration.
end note

note over PROV
  **A login must be nameable, or it cannot win.**
  The row listed variables only, so a remembered
  answer naming one outranked a login made later,
  and proveo forwarded a token the API refused
  while a working subscription sat mounted, unread
end note

note over AGENT
  **A login on disk is a credential that wins —**
  //while it can still authenticate.//
  Where the mounted home holds one, proveo injects
  no auth variable for that provider: an env token
  supersedes the file instead of joining it, which
  billed a subscription run as API usage.

  The qualifier is the whole risk: suppressing a
  working token in favour of a DEAD file leaves the
  run with no credential of any kind.
end note

note over AGENT
  **A file is not a login. A TOKEN is.**
  ""loginUsable"" judged the two expiry stamps only,
  so a record whose tokens had been taken away read
  as healthy-but-stale — usable, needing a refresh
  that had nothing left to refresh with.

  **That is the ordinary state of the proveo home on
  macOS.** Running ""claude"" there moves the credential
  into the **Keychain** — which is machine-global, not
  per-""HOME"" — and rewrites ""\.credentials\.json"" with
  ""accessToken"" and ""refreshToken"" blanked while every
  stamp stays in place. Upstream calls this working as
  intended (anthropics/claude-code#10039, closed as not
  planned), so proveo must recognise the shape, not
  wait for it to stop happening.

  So the check reads the tokens for **emptiness**, never
  for value, and distinguishes ABSENT from BLANK: a field
  that was never there is a shape we do not judge, an
  explicit """" is a token that was removed. A blanked
  refresh token also voids the refresh branch — the stamp
  then describes a renewal that cannot happen.
end note

note over AGENT
  **Reading that file correctly is not the same as reading
  the right file.** ""hasPersistedLogin"" looks under the
  proveo home, and on the **sbx backend nothing does**: sbx
  runs its own agent user and its credential proxy writes
  the credential into THAT user's home from ""sbx secret"".
  The proveo home's copy is not consulted by the run at all
  — see ""_spec/internal/proveohome/proveo-home-lifecycle\.puml"",
  where moving ""HOME"" onto it orphaned the live credential.

  So on macOS both halves were right and the answer was
  wrong: the file is a husk, ""tokenCleared"" says "no login",
  and proveo REFUSED the run — "needs a subscription login
  and the sbx backend cannot complete one" — while
  ""CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN (stored)"" and
  ""anthropic (oauth configured)"" sat in the store the whole
  time. Every new shell was told to export a token it did
  not need.

  ""sbxStoredAuth"" adds the second store, gated on actually
  reaching that backend. What it learns is **weaker** and is
  said as such: ""sbx secret ls"" prints a name and "(stored)",
  so an entry holding "" "" looks exactly like a live token.
  Presence decides whether to LAUNCH; it never licenses a
  claim about the value. ""sbx\.Available"" is the probe rather
  than ""ensureSbx"" — this runs before the backend is chosen
  and must install nothing and ask nothing.
end note

note over AGENT
  **Suppression spans both backend renderings.**
  One predicate governs the docker argv and sbx
  Kit, since a credential resolved differently per
  rendering revives the divergence that a single
  host-side decision exists to end.

  **The predicate agreed; the rendering did not.** docker
  omitted a suppressed variable, sbx stated it as
  ""-e VAR="" — on the theory that sbx's global secret
  store would otherwise inject a stale value in front of
  the mounted login.

  //Probed and false.// With ""ANTHROPIC_API_KEY"" and
  ""CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN"" both in sbx v0\.39\.0's global
  store and no ""-e"" flag, both arrive **UNSET**: sbx
  attaches service secrets as proxy headers and writes the
  harness credential to ""~/\.claude/\.credentials\.json"".
  It never exports them as environment variables, so there
  was nothing in front of the login to override.

  **The empty value was the failure, not the guard.** An
  agent reads a SET variable as a chosen credential whatever
  it holds, and claudecode ranks both of these ABOVE the
  login on disk — so a blank one took the slot the login
  needed, and an unattended run stalled at a prompt asking
  it to approve a key that authenticates nothing. Both
  renderings now omit.
end note

note over AGENT
  **The mounted-"".env"" warning is tier-bound, and fires on ""open"" only.**
  With a "".env"" inside the mounted workspace //and// a provider key
  present, the agent reads the real key straight off the filesystem —
  and on the plain-bridge tier nothing at the network layer stops it
  from leaving. That is the exfil case the warning exists for.

  On the intercepting tiers (""allowlist"", ""review"") it is deliberately
  skipped: egress DLP plus the header-strip above blocks the exfil even
  though the agent can still read the file. A warning there would train
  the operator to ignore it.

  **It was dead for one release cycle.** The guard returned on all three
  canonical tiers, so it could not fire at all — a rename artifact: the
  tiers were ""broker"" / ""firewall"" / ""proxy"" when it was written, and
  the canonical rename swept ""open"" (then ""broker"") into the skip list
  with the two it was meant to skip. Found by migrating this prose into
  this diagram and reading the two against each other; fixed by dropping
  ""open"" from the switch.
end note

note over AGENT
  **The ""gh"" config directory is a deliberate exception to keeping host
  credentials out of the container.** ""ghConfigMount"" mounts
  ""$GH_CONFIG_DIR"" (else ""~/\.config/gh"") so ""gh"" works inside the
  agent, and ""hosts\.yml"" there holds an OAuth token the agent can read.

  The mount is **read-only**, and read-only prevents //rewriting// the
  host's credential, not //reading// it. What bounds the damage is the
  container boundary and the egress allowlist above — not the mount flag.

  The intent is reuse rather than issuance: ""gh"" inside the container picks
  up the session the operator already holds instead of asking for a token of
  its own.

  Opt out with ""PROVEO_MOUNT_GH_CONFIG=0"".
end note

note over RUN
  **What actually crosses on ""docker run"", per declared variable.** A
  non-secret is forwarded //by name// — a bare ""-e NAME"", whose value docker
  copies out of ""proveo""'s own process environment. A secret declared in
  ""env:"" either forwards its real value (""--credentials forward"") or is
  replaced by the ""proveo-brokered"" sentinel and named in
  ""PROVEO_CREDENTIAL_BROKER_KEYS"", so the entrypoint can tell a sentinel
  from a key. Every other provider key present but //not// declared by the
  manifest is sentinel-injected the same way, which is what stops an
  undeclared key from arriving as a working credential.

  //The tier names in the removed prose are history.// It read "broker
  forwards the real value; firewall injects the sentinel; proxy withholds" —
  from before the axis split, when the network tier and credential handling
  were one flag. Withholding a declared variable entirely is not a shape the
  code has: below ""forward"" the variable still arrives, holding the
  sentinel.
end note
@enduml
